st it's relentless and  it's and it's and it's  kind of plotting and ponderous and and  uh  uh and so it's uh but i think there was  some emulation  hidden emulation there so you know in  the essay  he says not only the jews are alien to  german culture but he also implies that  they're  incapable of producing real art right  that they can only ever  imitate or kind of parody and you know  so there's a question  um about whether you know that's that's  definitely a trope that you see in later  anti-semites  so like you know i'm thinking of someone  like dramaal you know at the end of the  19th century  um and certainly you know into the 20s  so but is that do you know whether  that's  new with wagner is he that's not new i  mean uh franz brendell  uh the editor of the neue et cetera for  music uh had  had said the same thing in an earlier  essay  i think also ted or uliq another  especially character ulik  rather than brendel had said really made  the same  claim about jewish musicians jewish  composers  in in earlier essay that had set off  this debate  then there were there were there was  there was another critic ludwig bischoff  who was defending uh mendelssohn and and  my beer and saying this  this anti-jewishness is is ridiculous  and  and um uh instead of heading back and  that degree and so that's where  wagner stepped in um but no this this  point had already been made specifically  about  uh jewish composers but but wagner  fleshed it out certainly uh he went on  at greater length um and and he  that that vehemence that that  repetitiveness  uh kind of solidified this idea was no  longer something that had been thrown  out in passing  in the middle of this back and forth  debate uh  uh in fighting among kind of german  music critics and commentators  uh it became it's the essay was just  long enough that it became a statement  on its own um and people forgot about  the the  the context of the various other  politics back and forth especially when  it was r